I was a bit worried that someone had stolen my idea for Nethack as IF
(seeing as people have done Quake, Hunt the Wumpus and Centipede as
IF), but you seem to be after something else. (*)

> I guess initial development comes down to how well an if-game language and
> the Nethack engine work together. How object-oriented (OOPy) is the Nethack
> code and does it follow the model view controller (MVC) design pattern?

I haven't done much source diving, but nethack doesn't have much in
the way of object orientation: it's written in C, and has kind of
accumulated over the years rather than being designed with an overall
plan. On the other hand, the display code is fairly separated from the
game engine. Not exactly what I would call MVC, but quite flexible -
enough to do things like Falcon's Eye or noegnud.

> Things will be very difficult if the nethack source is not OOPY and if the
> map and user input are tightly coupled with the engine. also, I don't know
> if any IF-languages support C or CPP directly. in the extreme nnightmare
> scenario, one would have to port the whole of nethack apart from the map and
> the user input into Tads or some other interactive fiction language. My
> original idea was that this could be a rather quick and easy conversion
> given no engine porting was necessary.

AFAIK, no IF language uses C, so that would mean porting the nethack
engine as you say.

The easiest way of doing what you want would be to add a new
interface, built on the console version, but with separate output for
the inventory, prompts, game messages and so on. That shouldn't be too
hard - you could hijack the output, send what you think is interesting
to a screen reader, and send it on to the screen. That way it would
look the same as the console version, but with additional support for
speech.

Adding speech command/information shouldn't be impossible, but I think
you might have to do some parsing of the output to get what you want.
In your place I would check out the display code and see how much you
could do with that.

